% ============================================================================
% fossil-preservation-subtype — a named SUB-CATEGORY of one of
% `fossil-preservation-type.adj`'s three preservation structures, where the
% same source defines one (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `fossil-preservation-type.adj`
% (`fossil_preservation_type(type, description)`, THREE peer-level
% preservation structures: mold, cast, trace_fossil): a new
% `fossil_preservation_subtype(subtype, parent_type, description)` table
% names a SPECIFIC KIND of one of those three structures, decoded from a
% span already sitting unused inside that table's own header — no new
% WebFetch. `fossil-preservation-type.adj`'s header already quotes, in full,
% the National Park Service's own definition of a `steinkern`, but
% deliberately does not table it as a fourth peer-level row: the SAME source
% page frames a steinkern as a SPECIFIC KIND of `cast` (an internal cast),
% not a fourth preservation structure standing alongside mold/cast/
% trace_fossil. That is a classificatory/scope decision, not a correctness
% one — the fact is fully grounded, it just does not fit that table's
% single-axis schema. This table gives it its own axis: subtype → parent
% type.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? fossil_preservation_subtype(steinkern, $ParentType, $Description)
%       % cast, stony_cast_of_the_interior_of_a_shell_or_other_hollow_organic_structure_made_by_sediment_infilling
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a THREE-column table like
% `animal-baby-sex.adj`'s own `animal_baby_sex`): each `row` lowers to a
% ground relation `fossil_preservation_subtype(subtype, parent_type,
% description)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary
% SLD binding query — the engine returns the parent type and description
% WITH the source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% term that is not itself a named sub-category (`mold`, `cast`, and
% `trace_fossil` are the three PEER types this table's parent already
% covers — recalling them here, rather than as a subtype, honestly
% abstains, since none of them is itself a sub-category of another).
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a parent type and recall which
% sub-category belongs to it:
%
%     ? fossil_preservation_subtype($Subtype, cast, $Description)   % steinkern
%
% Only ONE sub-category is grounded in the cited page's own definitions;
% this table is deliberately narrow rather than inventing sub-categories
% the source does not define with their own clean, standalone sentence.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME steinkern definition
% already quoted inside `fossil-preservation-type.adj`'s own header, from
% the SAME National Park Service "Mold Casts and Steinkerns" article that
% table already cites — no new WebFetch, only a different schema for a fact
% that table's own header had already verified but structurally excluded.
% `trust authoritative` — the same tier `fossil-preservation-type.adj`
% already carries (National Park Service, a primary U.S. government
% source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table fossil_preservation_subtype {
    columns subtype, parent_type, description

    row (steinkern, cast, stony_cast_of_the_interior_of_a_shell_or_other_hollow_organic_structure_made_by_sediment_infilling)

    source "A stony three-dimensional cast of the interior of a shell or other hollow organic structure made by sediment infilling prior to the shell being dissolved."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/mold-casts-and-steinkerns.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
